<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Tap host entitlements.

  App Group — write SharedTapState into the Group Container the
  TapWidgets.appex reads. ID must be team-prefixed
  (F6ZAL7ANAD.group.com.mattssoftware.tap) or containermanagerd
  rejects access at runtime. (Group id stays `.tap`, not
  `.tap.macos`, so the existing widget extension and the host
  find the same container — App Groups are a separate registration
  from the primary bundle id.)

  Sign in with Apple — NOT achievable on this distribution path.
  Confirmed via four separate provisioning-profile generations
  (three through the portal, one via the App Store Connect API
  with kid YRR5D454TC) that Apple's `MAC_APP_DIRECT` Developer ID
  profile type *never* carries the applesignin entitlement in
  its `Entitlements` dict, even when the App ID has APPLE_ID_AUTH
  on as a primary. And amfid hard-rejects any Developer ID-
  signed Mac binary that claims `com.apple.developer.applesignin`
  unless an embedded profile authorizes it — with or without a
  profile embedded, the binary fails to launch with POSIX 163
  "Launchd job spawn failed". This is a platform constraint;
  the only macOS distribution channel that does carry SIWA in
  the profile entitlements is `MAC_APP_STORE` (i.e. the Mac App
  Store), and that requires App Store Connect submission rather
  than Developer ID + notarytool.

  Paths forward if SIWA on Tap-for-Mac becomes important:
    A. Submit Tap to the Mac App Store. Profile type
       `MAC_APP_STORE` (App Store profile) does carry the
       applesignin entitlement; the cert is Apple Distribution
       instead of Developer ID Application; release artefact is
       a .pkg/.app uploaded to ASC instead of a .dmg.
    B. Swap `ASAuthorizationAppleIDProvider` for an
       `ASWebAuthenticationSession` flow against
       `appleid.apple.com/auth/authorize`. Doesn't need the
       platform entitlement, just regular OAuth from a web view.
       Server side needs to handle the OAuth callback.
    C. Use a non-Apple sign-in (Sign in with Google /
       email + password) — sidesteps SIWA entirely.

  Until one of those lands, stays out of the entitlements file
  and the SignInView's button will fail with the SiwA error
  1000 we've been seeing.

  NOT sandboxed: the SSH execute path goes through
  `tap.mattssoftware.com` over HTTPS but the host also stores the
  bearer token in the login Keychain, and SIWA's
  `ASAuthorizationAppleIDProvider` plays nicer outside the sandbox.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>F6ZAL7ANAD.group.com.mattssoftware.tap</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
